        ":question/id": "d2a5442d-2a2e-5ccb-8bcf-80decc5dbd6d",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/problem": "Calculate the definite integral $\\int_{-1}^{0}\\frac{2 x^{2}}{2 x^{3} + 9}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =2 x^{3} + 9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =6 x^{2} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =6 x^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u & =2 x^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2 x^{3} + 9.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & -1 & 0 \\\\ u & 7 & 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{0}\\frac{2 x^{2}}{2 x^{3} + 9}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int_{7}^{9}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{7}^{9}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{7}^{9}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\ln(u)|_{7}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\ln(9)-\\ln(7)) \\\\  & =- \\log{\\left(7 \\right)} + \\frac{2 \\log{\\left(21 \\right)}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
