        ":question/problem": "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n$\\int_{2}^{4}\\frac{10 x}{\\sqrt{(5 x^{2} - 1)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =5 x^{2} - 1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =10 x \\\\ \\text{d}u & =10 x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=5 x^{2} - 1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 2 & 4 \\\\ u & 19 & 79\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{2}^{4}\\frac{10 x}{\\sqrt{(5 x^{2} - 1)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int_{19}^{79}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{19}^{79}1u^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u & =- \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{u}}|_{19}^{79} \\\\  & =-2\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{79}}+2\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{19}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{19}}{19}-2\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{79}} \\\\  & =- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{79}}{79} + \\frac{2 \\sqrt{19}}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
