        ":question/problem": "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n$\\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{12 x}{\\sqrt{(4 x^{2} + 6)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =4 x^{2} + 6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =8 x \\\\ \\text{d}u & =8 x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=4 x^{2} + 6.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 2 \\\\ u & 6 & 22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{12 x}{\\sqrt{(4 x^{2} + 6)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int_{6}^{22}\\frac{\\frac{3}{2}}{\\sqrt{u^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{6}^{22}\\frac{3}{2}u^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u & =- \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{u}}|_{6}^{22} \\\\  & =-3\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{22}}+3\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}-3\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{22}} \\\\  & =- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{22}}{22} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
